        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: tatsächlich",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tatsächlich — \"in fact, actually; real\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ich dachte, der Laden ist zu. Er war aber tatsächlich bis acht Uhr offen. I thought the shop was closed. In fact, it was open until eight o'clock."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "As an answer: Tatsächlich? — Really? As an adjective: der tatsächliche Preis — the real price."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tatsächlich is built on Tatsache \"fact\" (EtymDB), from Tat \"deed\", Old High German tāt, and Sache \"thing, matter\", Old High German sahha. Tatsache is usually said to have been coined in the 18th century as a rendering of English matter of fact."
            }
          ]
        },
